trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Serum albumin is severely low (1.8 g/dL; normal 3.5–5.0 g/dL), which strongly explains both bilateral leg edema (due to reduced oncotic pressure) and fatigue (often seen in hypoalbuminemia from malnutrition, chronic inflammation, or liver disease). This finding shifts the diagnostic focus toward identifying the cause of hypoalbuminemia. Key possibilities include:  
- Chronic malnutrition (plausible in long-term smoker with chronic bronchitis — may have reduced intake, increased metabolic demand, or COPD-related cachexia);  
- Liver synthetic dysfunction (though no jaundice, ascites, or prior liver disease reported);  
- Protein-losing enteropathy or nephropathy (but no diarrhea, foamy urine, or renal symptoms reported);  
- Chronic inflammation or malignancy (44-pack-year smoking increases lung cancer risk — fatigue and edema can be paraneoplastic).  

Next, I need liver function tests (ALT, AST, ALP, total bilirubin) to assess hepatic synthetic capacity and rule out liver disease as the cause of low albumin. Also, creatinine/eGFR is essential to exclude nephrotic syndrome (which causes hypoalbuminemia + edema) — though the patient denied foamy urine, subclinical proteinuria can occur.

[TEST] Measure serum creatinine and calculate estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR).

